---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'

const entries = await getCollection('articles')
const sorted = [...entries].sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.datePublished.valueOf() - a.data.datePublished.valueOf()
    || a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title),
)
const fmt = (d: Date) => d.toISOString().slice(0, 10)
---
<Base
  title="Articles — LunaTrack"
  description="Sourced articles on cycle tracking, predictions, and reading a period tracker's privacy policy, each carrying its own dated citations."
  path="/articles"
>
  <h1 class="display text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.6rem]">Articles</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose">
    Longer pieces than a calculator result or a features list, each one written to
    stand on its own and each one carrying dated, verifiable sources at the end
    rather than asking you to take a claim on faith. Every citation links out to
    the actual reference — NHS pages, Cleveland Clinic, ACOG, the ICO — so you can
    check a claim against the source yourself instead of trusting a summary of it.
    Some articles cover the mechanics behind what the app and the calculators on
    this site actually do, such as how a prediction is built from your own
    averages, and some step back to give you a way to evaluate any tracker's
    privacy practices, including LunaTrack's own, rather than only describing
    what this app chooses to do. More will be added over time.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose">
    One thing is true of every article here and worth stating plainly rather than
    burying in fine print: none of them has been reviewed by a clinician. They are
    written and checked against the sources listed at the end of each one, and
    each article's page says so in a visible notice near the top rather than
    quietly implying a medical review that never happened — the same honesty
    standard the structured data behind each page follows, since a page's schema
    only ever names a reviewer when one genuinely reviewed it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose">
    None of these replace the calculators — see the
    <a href="/tools" class="underline">cycle calculators</a> if you want a quick
    estimate from your own dates instead of background reading — and none of them
    replace a doctor or midwife for anything specific to your own health.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-10 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-9 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {sorted.map((entry) => (
      <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
        <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.3rem]">
          <a href={`/articles/${entry.id}`} class="underline">{entry.data.title}</a>
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm">{entry.data.description}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs opacity-70">
          Published <time datetime={fmt(entry.data.datePublished)}>{fmt(entry.data.datePublished)}</time>
        </p>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>
